  "worked_solution": "The function has a discontinuity at $x=-2.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(-2)=0$ exists $\\,\u2713$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)=1$ exists $\\,\u2713$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)\\ne f(-2)\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=-2$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nNote that the function also has a discontinuity at $x=2.$ but it is not a point discontinuity. Checking the conditions, we find that\n\n- $f(2)=0$ exists $\\,\u2713$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\times $\n\nSo this time, the second condition failed, and therefore $x=2$ is not a point discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, the correct answer is $x=-2$ only.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
